 "observations": "In this run we observed a session-1 build of a 14-module package whose module docstrings name each module's 'Secret' (1.15, 1.48). Later sessions began by reading most or all modules before editing (2.4-2.6, 3.3-3.10, 4.3-4.11, 6.3-6.13). Session 5 was the exception: it read selectively (5.4, 5.6). New features went mostly into new modules: flow/navigation (2.12, 2.16), signatures/symbols (4.23, 4.28), refactoring/diagnostics (5.14, 5.16), config/environments/interpreter (6.16-6.22). Session 2 instead rewrote inference.py wholesale (2.14). Private cross-module access appeared in session 2 (navigation uses inference._DictView, 2.46) and session 5 (refactoring uses navigation._Follower and _arguments). Session 5 extracted occurrences_of() so that rename and references share matching (5.32). Verification relied on /tmp probes in every session. Session 2 also installed jedi and diffed against it (2.9, 2.42). From session 2 on, the agent wrote a stdlib unittest suite and re-ran it at each later checkpoint (3.31, 4.14, 5.13, 6.14). Session 1 wrote DESIGN.md (1.48). Of 17 first failures, 13 were coded spec-silent. For example, the env probe protocol did not match the test's fake interpreters (ck6), and the ck3 rule that goto stops at imports conflicted with the ck4 stub-goto tests. Two were coded spec-misread: the circular-import visibility rule (ck3) and smart_sys_path not adding __init__.py directories (ck6). No regressions occurred."
